# Target C — finite Oler sandwich for the Section-116 outer detour

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**  
**Status:** proof-only research lemma; theorem authority unchanged.

This note attacks the full-core Route-A quantity from the current Target-C handoff.
For the same harmonic attachment cell used in the Section-116 zero-host theorem, a
finite Oler calculation gives an upper bound for the exact outer-walk detour

\[
E_{\rm out}:=L+P_{\rm exc}-2d.
\]

Combined with the hostile-audited Section-116 lower bound, this produces a two-sided
finite inequality on one actual source cell. No source point is deleted and no
full-source ZR/ZF statement is used.

The result does **not** close C-II: it shows precisely that Route A still needs a
boundary-order bound on the local non-rhombic area. Type II currently gives only an
`o(D^2)` bound for that area on a macroscopic carrier, which is insufficient at the
linear scale of the detour theorem.

---

## 1. Setup

Fix one canonical harmonic component `I` and its distinct loaded attachment set `A`.
Use the local notation of canonical Sections 62, 82, and 89:

- `n=|I|`;
- `a=|A|`;
- `P=conv(A)`;
- `p=per(P)` and `d=diam(P)`;
- `s` is the local pinned-contact surplus;
- `c` is the number of loaded-loaded unit contacts with both endpoints in `A`;
- `L` is the outer-face walk length of the induced local contact graph;
- `f` is the number of bounded nonquadrilateral faces;
- `Q=sum_F(deg F-4)` over those faces;
- `R_4` is the number of bounded quadrilateral faces;
- `A_4` is their total Euclidean area; and
- `P_exc=4f+Q` is the total boundary length of the bounded nonquadrilateral faces,
  counted by edge occurrence.

Every quadrilateral face is a strict unit rhombus. Put

\[
a_0={\sqrt3\over2},\qquad c_0={\sqrt3\over4}={a_0\over2},
\]

and define its total local rhombus-area excess

\[
E_4^{\rm loc}:=A_4-a_0R_4\ge0.
\]

Finally set

\[
G:=\operatorname{area}(P)-A_4\ge0.
\]

Thus `G` is the entire area of the attachment polygon not occupied by its bounded
strict-rhombus faces. It includes bounded nonquadrilateral faces and any part of the
local outer face lying inside `P`.

Canonical Section 82 gives the exact finite identities

\[
R_4=n+2+s+c-a-f,
\tag{1.1}
\]

and

\[
L+Q=4a-6-2s-2c.
\tag{1.2}
\]

Hence

\[
E_{\rm out}
=4a-6-2s-2c+4f-2d.
\tag{1.3}
\]

---

## 2. Oler upper bound on the same detour

### Theorem 2.1 — finite local Oler detour upper bound

For every such harmonic cell,

\[
\boxed{
E_{\rm out}
\le
p-2d+2f+{2\over a_0}
\left(G+E_4^{\rm loc}\right).
}
\tag{2.1}
\]

Moreover every bounded nonquadrilateral face is convex, so Oler on its boundary
vertices gives

\[
G\ge a_0f+c_0Q\ge a_0f.
\tag{2.2}
\]

Consequently

\[
\boxed{
E_{\rm out}
\le
p-2d+{4\over a_0}G+{2\over a_0}E_4^{\rm loc}.
}
\tag{2.3}
\]

### Proof

Harmonic trapping puts every point of `I` in `P`, and of course every attachment lies
in `P`. Apply finite Oler to the `n+a` unit-separated source points `I union A` inside
the convex polygon `P`:

\[
a_0(n+a-1)\le \operatorname{area}(P)+c_0p.
\tag{2.4}
\]

From (1.1),

\[
n=R_4-2-s-c+a+f.
\]

Substitution in (2.4) gives

\[
a_0R_4+a_0(2a+f-s-c-3)
\le \operatorname{area}(P)+c_0p.
\]

Since `a_0R_4=A_4-E_4^{loc}`,

\[
a_0(2a+f-s-c-3)
\le G+E_4^{\rm loc}+c_0p.
\tag{2.5}
\]

On the other hand (1.3) is exactly

\[
E_{\rm out}
=2(2a+f-s-c-3)+2f-2d.
\tag{2.6}
\]

Multiply (2.5) by `2/a_0`, use `2c_0/a_0=1`, and insert into (2.6). This proves
(2.1).

For (2.2), let a bounded nonquadrilateral face have degree `4+r`, `r>=1`, and area
`A_F`. Its boundary vertices are unit-separated and the face is convex by the
canonical local harmonic-cell geometry. Oler applied only to those `4+r` boundary
vertices gives

\[
4+r\le {A_F\over a_0}+{4+r\over2}+1,
\]

so

\[
A_F\ge a_0+{a_0\over2}r=a_0+c_0r.
\]

Summing over the `f` exceptional bounded faces gives total exceptional-face area at
least `a_0f+c_0Q`. Their interiors are disjoint and none is counted in `A_4`, hence
that total area is at most `G`. This proves (2.2). Substituting
`2f<=2G/a_0` into (2.1) gives (2.3). `square`

---

## 3. Exact sandwich with Section 116

The hostile-audited proof-only Section-116 two-arc theorem applies to this same finite
cell and gives

\[
\boxed{
dE_{\rm out}\ge(4-2\sqrt3)A_4.}
\tag{3.1}
\]

Combining (2.1) and (3.1) yields the finite source-level sandwich

\[
\boxed{
{(4-2\sqrt3)A_4\over d}
\le E_{\rm out}
\le p-2d+2f+{2\over a_0}(G+E_4^{\rm loc}).
}
\tag{3.2}
\]

Using (2.3) gives the simpler but weaker form

\[
\boxed{
{(4-2\sqrt3)A_4\over d}
\le
p-2d+{4\over a_0}G+{2\over a_0}E_4^{\rm loc}.
}
\tag{3.3}
\]

Equivalently, whenever the bracket is positive,

\[
\boxed{
G+E_4^{\rm loc}
\ge {a_0\over2}
\left(
{(4-2\sqrt3)A_4\over d}-p+2d-2f
\right).
}
\tag{3.4}
\]

This is not an asymptotic comparison between different cells: every term in (3.2)--
(3.4) belongs to the same finite harmonic attachment cell.

---

## 4. Hostile scope audit

The proof above deliberately avoids the recurrent Target-C scope failures.

1. **No source deletion.** Oler is applied to the subset `I union A`; ignoring other
   source points can only weaken an upper packing bound and does not alter the source.
2. **Contact isolates are retained globally.** Isolates lying in `P` are not silently
   identified with core vertices. Their possible occupied area is exactly one reason
   why `G` is not presently controlled at boundary order.
3. **ZR/ZF is not extended to isolated coordinates.** The proof uses only canonical
   local face identities and harmonic trapping for the nonisolated component.
4. **Repeated outer-walk occurrences and bridges are retained.** They are already
   built into `L`; `P_exc` is occurrence-counted exactly as in Sections 82 and 116.
5. **No near-equality-to-quadrangulation inference is used.** `R_4`, `f`, and `Q` are
   exact face quantities.
6. **The Section-116 lower bound remains proof-only.** The new upper bound (2.1) is
   independent of Section 116; only the sandwich (3.2) uses that proof-only input.

---

## 5. What the theorem changes, and why it does not yet close Route A

The full-source slack theorem gives global strict-rhombus area excess `E_4=O(D)`, so
`E_4^{loc}=O(D)` for every cell. The genuinely uncontrolled term in (3.3) is therefore

\[
\boxed{G=\operatorname{area}(P)-A_4.}
\]

For the macroscopic wrapping carrier, current Type-II saturation gives only

\[
G=o(D^2).
\]

That is too weak for (3.3), whose lower and other upper terms are all linear in `D`.
A bound such as `G=O(D)` with a sufficiently sharp coefficient would turn (3.2) into
an actual Route-A comparison. A merely subquadratic estimate does not.

This identifies a sharper next fork than another carrier-decimal improvement:

- prove a boundary-order bound on the non-rhombic area of the actual wrapping carrier;
- or show that a superlinear `G` forces a mesoscopic inactive container/interface that
  itself contradicts global diameter minimality;
- or bypass `G` by an all-regime signed-return theorem that charges physical route
  occurrences without converting unfilled area into detour budget.

No theorem authority is changed and no C-II closure is claimed.

